1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.838
Welcome to Improving Accuracy of LLM
Applications.

2
00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:08.908
Built in partnership with Lamini and Meta.
AI applications

3
00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:12.412
can now perform tasks that were previously
very challenging for computers,

4
00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:16.249
such as providing a natural language
interface to a database.

5
00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:21.783
But these applications often perform well
in some areas and yet struggle in others.

6
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:26.559
In this course, you learn a framework
for a set of development steps

7
00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:30.463
to systematically improve
your application's performance.

8
00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:35.635
Specifically, you create an evaluation
dataset to measure performance,

9
00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:40.206
prompt engineer,
and then finally fine-tune your model.

10
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.343
I'm delighted to introduce our instructors
for this course.

11
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.280
Sharon Zhou is a returning instructor
and CEO of Lamini,

12
00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:50.550
which is a company
that provides LLM serving and fine

13
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.120
tuning for their users,
and in which I made a seed investment.

14
00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:57.724
And another returning instructor,
Amit Sangani,

15
00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:01.728
is Director of partnerships
of the wonderful Llama team at Meta.

16
00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.564
Sharon and Amit. Welcome back.

17
00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.499
Thanks so much.
And you're excited to be back.

18
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.734
Thank you Andrew.

19
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.537
So Amit, the Llama family of open

20
00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.540
source models has become a huge success.

21
00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.141
Indeed Andrew.

22
00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.511
The Llama models are widely used
in numerous applications.

23
00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.280
They have undergone

24
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.551
extensive training and rank highly
in most foundation model benchmarks.

25
00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.954
It's gratifying
to see so many organizations

26
00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:31.191
using our models,
but it also presents challenges.

27
00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:34.561
Since the models are trained on public
data sets and tasks,

28
00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:38.765
they may not perform as well
on applications requiring proprietary data

29
00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:42.402
or on tasks
that weren't specifically trained for.

30
00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:45.472
But Meta has released
these models as open-source,

31
00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:50.705
and this allows any developer to fine
tune them for their specific applications.

32
00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.312
Exactly, Andrew.

33
00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:55.582
A crucial aspect of these models
is their open-source nature.

34
00:01:56.216 --> 00:02:00.086
This means users can fine-tune
the models for their specific tasks.

35
00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:04.157
We have seen many applications,
especially with the smaller models

36
00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:08.528
where users have fine
tuned them for tasks like text to SQL

37
00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:13.233
classification, question and answers,
recommendation, and summarization.

38
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:17.170
They've have also been adapted to understand
proprietary datasets

39
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.607
such as financial data, customer
data, and legal information.

40
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:25.979
I really appreciate Meta's work
releasing these models in an open way.

41
00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:30.583
And yet, fine-tuning also isn't
the very first step

42
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:34.921
we typically recommend to application
builders is one of the options

43
00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:39.759
that we might pursue only after exhausting
other, simpler options.

44
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.595
That's right Andrew,
and we'll go through exactly that.

45
00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.229
At Lamini

46
00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:47.734
we work with many enterprises
to improve the accuracy of their LLM

47
00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:52.734
applications, specifically to make
LLMS factual and precise, and where users

48
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.444
can start talking about nines
in their LLM accuracy, like 95%, 99%.

49
00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.980
We've observed a successful pattern

50
00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.983
that has inspired
the outline of this course for you.

51
00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.053
First
you'll build your LLM application, hook it

52
00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:10.056
all up, prompt engineer
with some self-reflection.

53
00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:15.190
Next, it's important to get rigorous
in evaluating the model's performance

54
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:20.099
so you know whether it's ready
for prime time and where you can tell it

55
00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:23.503
to actually improve
where the next frontier of accuracy is.

56
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.206
If this meets your needs, then great.

57
00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:29.475
But if you find that even after
a lot of prompt engineering work, it's

58
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.844
still not yet working accurately enough.

59
00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:36.115
The next step is to use LLMs and create
a dataset for fine-tuning your model.

60
00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:40.153
One myth of this step is thinking
you don't have enough data.

61
00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:44.691
You'll actually learn that you probably do
have enough data, and there are ways

62
00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:49.495
to significantly amplify
the data you do have with LLMs themselves.

63
00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:55.163
Fine-tuning also used to be slow and
costly, but by using parameter efficient

64
00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:59.672
fine-tuning techniques like LoRA,
which stands for Low Rank adaptation,

65
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.875
the time and costs have dropped
dramatically.

66
00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:05.878
What's more,
Lamini memory tuning is a technique

67
00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:09.015
that gives you a new frontier
of factual accuracy,

68
00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:13.519
reducing time and developer effort to get
to the same level or higher accuracy.

69
00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:18.658
Using optimized fine-tuning techniques,
you can teach your LLM thousand new facts

70
00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:23.396
in just minutes on a single A100
or MI2 50 GPU.

71
00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:27.267
In this course,
we'll use an example of fine-tuning an LLM

72
00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.470
to generate SQL queries
for a specific schema.

73
00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:35.241
Through fine-tuning, you'll see accuracy
improved from something more like 30%,

74
00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:40.241
all the way up to 95%, using
just a small data set of 128 examples

75
00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:44.083
in about 30 minutes,
at a cost of just a few dollars.

76
00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:48.021
What's more,
that process can be further optimized down

77
00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:51.791
to 6 or 7 seconds with even
higher performance memory tuning.

78
00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:55.261
Many people have contributed to
this course.

79
00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:59.165
I'd like to thank from Lamini, Johnny
Li and Nina Wei.

80
00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:03.670
And from DeepLearning.AI
Geoff Ladwig and Esmaeil Gargari.

81
00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.040
All right, let's dive into optimizing

82
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.209
applications
by going on to the next video.

